###############################################################################
# MAL redirect-URI probes
#
# Manual companion to ticket 02 (`.scratch/mal-auth-redirect/issues/02-probe-
# redirect-uri-forms.md`) and §1.2 of `../mal-auth-implementation.md`. Registration
# on myanimelist.net/apiconfig still needs a browser; everything a machine *can*
# check is here.
#
# Written for the JetBrains HTTP Client (IntelliJ / Fleet). `# @no-redirect`,
# `# @no-cookie-jar` and the `> {% %}` response handlers are its syntax — the VS
# Code REST Client will still fire the requests but ignores all three.
#
# Fill in `@clientId` below (a runtime value, never committed) and run the
# sections in order.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# URLs to test the redirect to
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every URI that has to be registered on the one MAL app, in the order ticket 02
# probes them. Record the outcome of each of the three checks; §1.2 of the
# research doc wants the results written back into it, replacing `[unverified]`.
#
#   | # | Redirect URI                                  | Target                  | apiconfig | authorize | token    |
#   |---|-----------------------------------------------|-------------------------|-----------|-----------|----------|
#   | 1 | io.challenge-workshop.malui://oauth/callback  | Android custom scheme   | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#   | 2 | http://127.0.0.1:18040/oauth/callback         | Desktop loopback, IP    | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#   | 3 | http://localhost:18040/oauth/callback         | Desktop loopback, name  | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#   | 4 | https://mal-ui.localhost/oauth/callback       | Web wasmJs, rev. proxy  | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#   | 5 | https://js.mal-ui.localhost/oauth/callback    | Web js, rev. proxy      | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#   | 6 | http://localhost:18020/oauth/callback         | Web wasmJs, dev server  | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#   | 7 | http://localhost:18030/oauth/callback         | Web js, dev server      | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#   | 8 | http://localhost:8080/oauth/callback          | Legacy paste-the-code   | accepted  | accepted  | accepted |
#
#   apiconfig — accepted by the `App Redirect URL` textarea and survives a save.
#   authorize — §A here: MAL shows the consent page rather than an error.
#   token     — §B here: needs no MAL session.
#
# SETTLED by the 2026-08-06 run, all eight registered on one app
# (`mal-redirect-result.log`):
#   - §1.1 — **several redirect URIs on one client ID works.** The 2020 staff
#     report of "something wrong with the redirection" is stale. The clean design
#     holds; the one-MAL-app-per-platform fallback is not needed.
#   - §1.2 — every form is accepted, including the two the research doc bet
#     against: the bare IP literal (so desktop gets the RFC 8252 §8.3-preferred
#     form and never needs the dual-address-family `localhost` bind) and the
#     `.localhost` subdomains, which Google, Entra and Slack all reject.
#   - Omitting `redirect_uri` at authorize is now **illegal** — A9 went 303 → 401
#     the moment the set grew past one. That is what makes
#     `MalAuthConfig.redirectUri` non-null in ticket 09.
#
# 3 is redundant now that 2 is accepted, but it is registered and harmless. 8 is
# the historical paste-the-code URI, kept as a control.
#
# Two things to expect, both now confirmed rather than inherited from §1.3:
#   - Matching is byte-exact, and stricter than RFC 3986: MAL does not lowercase
#     the host and does not normalize the default port. No trailing slash, no case
#     change, no extra query parameter, no loopback port leniency. §C proves each.
#   - A mismatch reports as HTTP 401 `invalid_client` / "Client authentication
#     failed", which misleadingly implicates the client ID.
#
# `plain` PKCE means the verifier below travels in the authorization URL, so
# treat this file's output as sensitive: don't paste a real `code` or a real
# `access_token` into a ticket.
###############################################################################

### Variables

# Client ID from myanimelist.net/apiconfig. App Type `other` → no secret.
@clientId = xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# Leave empty unless the app was registered as App Type `web`.
@clientSecret =

# Fixed PKCE verifier and state. Under `plain`, code_challenge == code_verifier,
# so no hashing step: 43–128 unreserved characters is the only requirement.
@verifier = probe0verifier0probe0verifier0probe0verifier0probe
@state = probe-state-0001

# A code that is guaranteed invalid. §B relies on this: MAL validates the client
# and the redirect URI *before* the code, so a bad code still tells you whether
# the URI matched.
@badCode = definitely-not-a-real-authorization-code

@authorize = https://myanimelist.net/v1/oauth2/authorize
@token = https://myanimelist.net/v1/oauth2/token

# The URIs under test, same numbering as the table.
@uri1 = io.challenge-workshop.malui://oauth/callback
@uri2 = http://127.0.0.1:18040/oauth/callback
@uri3 = http://localhost:18040/oauth/callback
@uri4 = https://mal-ui.localhost/oauth/callback
@uri5 = https://js.mal-ui.localhost/oauth/callback
@uri6 = http://localhost:18020/oauth/callback
@uri7 = http://localhost:18030/oauth/callback
@uri8 = http://localhost:8080/oauth/callback

# Local services. `:server` is the relay; the two dev servers must serve
# /oauth/callback themselves; 18040 is the desktop loopback listener.
@relay = http://localhost:18010
@webWasm = http://localhost:18020
@webJs = http://localhost:18030
@proxyWasm = https://mal-ui.localhost
@proxyJs = https://js.mal-ui.localhost
@loopback = http://127.0.0.1:18040
# This machine's LAN address, for the §E binding check. Find it with `ip route get 1.1.1.1`.
@lanHost = 192.168.1.10

###############################################################################
# §A — authorize round trip, one per URI
#
# A form the registration page accepted can still fail here, which is why this
# section exists separately from apiconfig.
#
# Run these logged out and with the cookie jar off, so the response is about the
# request and not about your session. The two observed outcomes, from the
# 2026-08-06 run:
#   303 → myanimelist.net/login.php   ACCEPTED. MAL got past the client checks and
#                                     wants a login before showing consent.
#   401 invalid_client (JSON)         REJECTED. This URI is not registered on the
#                                     app (or the client ID is wrong).
# To see the actual consent screen, open the same URL in a logged-in browser; the
# no-redirect directive keeps this file from silently following the chain.
#
# `redirect_uri` is sent unencoded for readability. None of the eight values
# contains `&`, `=`, `+`, `#` or `%`, so there is nothing here that a query
# parser can misread; add percent-encoding if you extend the list with one that
# does.
###############################################################################

### A1 — Android custom scheme
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri1}}

> {%
    client.log(`A1 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A2 — Desktop loopback, IP literal
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri2}}

> {%
    client.log(`A2 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A3 — Desktop loopback, `localhost` name (only if A2 was rejected)
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri3}}

> {%
    client.log(`A3 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A4 — Web wasmJs behind the reverse proxy (`.localhost` subdomain)
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri4}}

> {%
    client.log(`A4 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A5 — Web js behind the reverse proxy
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri5}}

> {%
    client.log(`A5 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A6 — Web wasmJs dev server on :18020
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri6}}

> {%
    client.log(`A6 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A7 — Web js dev server on :18030
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri7}}

> {%
    client.log(`A7 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A8 — Legacy paste-the-code URI (control: this one is already registered)
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}&redirect_uri={{uri8}}

> {%
    client.log(`A8 status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

### A9 — No `redirect_uri` at all
# Legal only while exactly one URI is registered. CONFIRMED: 303 with one URI
# registered, 401 `invalid_client` with eight. This is the load-bearing check
# behind non-null `MalAuthConfig.redirectUri` in ticket 09 — and note it is the
# *authorize* endpoint that enforces it, not the token endpoint (see B0).
# Re-run it if the registered set is ever cut back to one.
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{authorize}}?response_type=code&client_id={{clientId}}&code_challenge={{verifier}}&code_challenge_method=plain&
    state={{state}}

> {%
    client.log(`A9 (no redirect_uri) status=${response.status} location=${response.headers.valueOf("Location") ?? "-"}`);
%}

###############################################################################
# §B — token endpoint exact-match probe, one per URI
#
# The decisive check, and the reason this file is worth having: it needs no MAL
# session and no browser. MAL validates client + redirect URI *before* it looks at
# the code — confirmed by the 2026-08-06 run — so an intentionally invalid code
# separates the two outcomes:
#
#   400 `invalid_request`  → the redirect URI MATCHED. MAL got as far as the code
#     hint "Cannot decrypt          and choked on it, which is the whole point.
#     the authorization code"       Note the code is `invalid_request`, not the
#                                   `invalid_grant` RFC 6749 §5.2 asks for.
#   401 `invalid_client`   → the redirect URI DID NOT match (or the client ID is
#     "Client authentication         wrong / the app is App Type `web` and wants a
#      failed"                       secret). Byte-identical body in every case,
#                                    so the status is the only signal.
#
# The check runs only when `redirect_uri` is present — B0 omits it and still gets
# the 400. Absent is not the same as mismatched here.
#
# So: run B0 first. If B0 is not a 400, the client credentials are the problem and
# every other result in this section is meaningless.
###############################################################################

### B0 — Control: no redirect_uri, so nothing but the credentials is under test
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{token}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

client_id = {{clientId}} &
client_secret = {{clientSecret}} &
grant_type = authorization_code &
code = {{badCode}} &
code_verifier = {{verifier}}

> {%
    const err = response.body?.error ?? "-";
    client.log(`B0 status=${response.status} error=${err}`);
    client.test("credentials are usable", () => {
        client.assert(response.status === 400,
            `expected 400 invalid_request, got ${response.status} ${err} — fix the client ID/secret before trusting B1..B8`);
    });
    // This is ONLY a credentials control. It stayed 400 with all eight URIs
    // registered, so the token endpoint validates redirect_uri only when it is
    // present — an omitted one is not an error here. A9 is the request that
    // proves omitting is illegal, and it does so at the authorize endpoint.
    // Whether the token endpoint enforces "same URI as authorize, or neither"
    // can only be seen with a real (decryptable) code — see §F.
%}

### B1 — Android custom scheme
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{token}}
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

client_id = {{clientId}} &
client_secret = {{clientSecret}} &
grant_type = authorization_code &
code = {{badCode}} &
code_verifier = {{verifier}} &
redirect_uri = {{uri1}}

> {%
    const err = response.body?.error ?? "-";
    client.log(`B1 status=${response.status} error=${err} → ${response.status === 400 ? "REGISTERED" : "NOT REGISTERED"}`);
%}

